WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.733
Bayes theorem.

2
00:00:02.033 --> 00:00:06.100
A very accurate detector can still raise mostly false alarms.

3
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:07.367
Bayes explains why.

4
00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:11.300
Bayes is a way of updating beliefs with evidence.

5
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.467
You start with a prior: how common is the thing at all?

6
00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:16.367
Then you observe some evidence.

7
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:19.700
And you end with a posterior: how likely is it now?

8
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.200
Here is the equation.

9
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:26.467
On the left, the posterior: the probability of A once we have seen B.

10
00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:31.133
On top, the likelihood, how well the evidence fits A, times the prior.

11
00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:33.733
Prior is how common A was to begin with.

12
00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:37.200
And we divide by how common the evidence is overall.

13
00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.165
Let's count.

14
00:00:39.165 --> 00:00:43.183
Imagine ten thousand stars, and only one percent have a planet.

15
00:00:43.183 --> 00:00:44.267
That's a hundred.

16
00:00:44.567 --> 00:00:46.953
The detector catches ninety five of them.

17
00:00:46.953 --> 00:00:48.233
These are true alarms.

18
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:53.375
But five percent of the other nine thousand nine hundred stars also trigger it.

19
00:00:53.375 --> 00:00:56.133
That's four hundred ninety five false alarms.

20
00:00:57.333 --> 00:00:58.567
Now compare the two.

21
00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:01.496
The false alarms dwarf the real planets.

22
00:01:01.496 --> 00:01:06.228
Out of five hundred ninety signals, only about sixteen percent are real.

23
00:01:06.228 --> 00:01:08.200
That is the base-rate fallacy.

24
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.833
Here is the same calculation in Python.

25
00:01:12.133 --> 00:01:16.133
We set the prior, the detector sensitivity, and the false alarm rate.

26
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:20.900
Then we compute the overall chance of seeing a signal, adding up both cases.

27
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:25.933
And Bayes gives the posterior: sixteen point one percent, not ninety five.

28
00:01:27.133 --> 00:01:29.300
This matters when you ship a classifier.

29
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.133
Your exoplanet model has ninety five percent recall and five percent false positives.

30
00:01:35.433 --> 00:01:38.833
But true transits are only about one percent of candidates.

31
00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:41.774
So only sixteen percent of alerts are real.

32
00:01:41.774 --> 00:01:46.133
Tune the threshold on precision at the real base rate, not on accuracy.

33
00:01:47.333 --> 00:01:48.125
Let's recap.

34
00:01:48.125 --> 00:01:50.500
Bayes updates beliefs with evidence.

35
00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.300
Posterior equals likelihood times prior, over evidence.

36
00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:57.100
When the event is rare, the prior dominates.

37
00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.286
So judge classifiers at the real base rate.

38
00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:02.500
Next up: conditional probability.
